==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|low and behold}} {{q|misspelling}}

===Etymology===
The {{m|en|lo}} in the expression probably originated from the shortening of the word {{m|en|look}}, commonly seen in {{der|en|enm|-}} texts. Its presence in literature can be traced to at least as early as the 18th century. The literal meaning of the expression is "look and see", and it is always used as if in the imperative.

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# {{non-gloss definition|Used to express surprise.}}
#* '''1766''', "Miss N", {{anchor|18thcent}}''[http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-loa1.htm#content Select letters Between the Late Duchess of Somerset, Lady Luxborough, Mr Whistler, ... and Others]'', Thomas Hull (editor)
#*: Here was I sat down, full of Love and Respect to write my dearest Friends a dutiful and loving letter, when '''lo, and behold'''! I was made happy by the receipt of yours.

